      <title type="html">The developer market trap for indies</title>
      <link href="https://tevinzhang.com/en/the-developer-market-trap-for-indies/" rel="alternate" type="text/html" title="The developer market trap for indies" />
      <published>2025-02-09T00:00:00+08:00</published>
      <updated>2026-01-25T22:50:08+08:00</updated>
      <id>https://tevinzhang.com/en/the-developer-market-trap-for-indies</id>
      
      

      
      
      
      
      

      <author>
          <name>Tevin Zhang</name>
        
          <email>hey(at)tevinzhang(dot)com</email>
        
        
      </author>

      
        <category term="blog" />
      

      

      
      
        <summary type="html"><![CDATA[You’ve likely heard the advice: “Start with your own needs”. This approach ensures authenticity, reduces risk, and allows you to build something you genuinely care about. But for indie developers1 whose customers are fellow techies, this approach may not always be ideal. I intentionally avoided the term “indie hackers” in this post because that will include creators that don’t involve software engineering, though I guess this applies to some of them as well, but not all. ↩]]></summary>
      

      
      
        
