      <title type="html">How I’ve managed notes over 12 years</title>
      <link href="https://tevinzhang.com/en/notes-over-12-years/" rel="alternate" type="text/html" title="How I’ve managed notes over 12 years" />
      <published>2024-08-15T00:00:00+08:00</published>
      <updated>2026-01-25T22:50:08+08:00</updated>
      <id>https://tevinzhang.com/en/notes-over-12-years</id>
      
      

      
      
      
      
      

      <author>
          <name>Tevin Zhang</name>
        
          <email>hey(at)tevinzhang(dot)com</email>
        
        
      </author>

      
        <category term="blog" />
      

      

      
      
        <summary type="html"><![CDATA[I have a Git repository dating back to 2012. Before that, I remember using Notational Velocity1 with a bunch of text files, it worked well, until I decided to keep my notes for life. If I’m not digitizing them, I’ll write notes on index cards and store them in boxes for organizing. As long as I can keep the boxes, I get to keep my notes. [Notational Velocity][5] is a text editor that lets me quickly search and switch between files. Later I switched to [nvALT][6]—a fork of Notational Velocity with some additional features and interface modifications, including MultiMarkdown functionality. ↩]]></summary>
      

      
      
        
